WEBVTT

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:44.240
The South Pacific.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:54.050
The name is familiar, but 230 years after Captain Cook's epic voyages,

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:57.170
this vast ocean remains little known.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:24.050
In the endless blue, isolated islands harbor life that's rarely seen, and these are some of the most pristine waters of any ocean.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:40.750
The turquoise seas and picture postcard islands look like heaven on earth.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:55.620
But all is not what it seems.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:00.000
The South Pacific also has an unforgiving

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:22.340
islands born from volcanic seas erupt with unique and extreme ways of life

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:30.000
people succeeded here again

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:44.660
the odds. The South Pacific is a tale of the unexpected, a forgotten world where

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:49.010
isolation has created the bizarre and surprising.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:18.110
This is the story of life in an ocean of islands.

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:24.180
Out of the blue, a giant emerges from the deep.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:29.890
Right now, the monster moves silently, but it's equipped with an awesome force.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.220
Some of the largest waves in the world break on South Pacific Islands.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:52.280
Their birth sums up the scale of this ocean.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:10.340
The storm swell that made these waves has traveled 3,000 miles to reach this shallow reef.

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:18.280
These distances proved a great challenge for animal castaways.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:22.920
But when they found new land, many evolved into new species.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:29.120
Human colonizers followed similar routes.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.900
The ocean that isolated them from the rest of the world became central to their culture.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:43.740
The big waves were an inspiration, and riding them has been a tradition here for more than

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.450
1500 years.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.880
ocean has had a greater impact on the lives of so many different animals and

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:46.860
cultures than the South Pacific and it's all down to its massive size the whole

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:52.340
Pacific Ocean is so large you could fit the world's continents inside it with

00:05:52.340 --> 00:06:00.000
nearly enough room for another Africa 10,000

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:11.400
miles wide less than 1% is land best known are the Hawaiian Islands they sit

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:19.150
1,500 miles north of the equator but their story was shaped by the South the

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:24.580
South Pacific is made up of thousands of islands united by cultures and bound

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:26.690
together by ocean currents

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:36.570
Most of the land sits in warm waters, but travel south and the character of the islands

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:38.070
change.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:45.150
New Zealand is not tropical but temperate.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:57.340
The ocean currents which cool New Zealand's waters occasionally bring icebergs.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:00.000
Starting in the Antarctic, they've drifted for nearly eight years.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:28.480
On the way, passing the most southerly outposts in the South Pacific, where New Zealand's temperate seas merge with the icy waters of the Southern Ocean, lies a solitary piece of land. 85 miles of wave-lashed coastline.

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:47.230
It looks desolate, but it's not deserted.

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:59.480
At certain times of year, there's more life here than on any other island in the Pacific.

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:17.570
In August, after months at sea, elephant seals arrive.

00:08:17.570 --> 00:08:26.340
They depend on this island for breeding.

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:30.000
Macquarie will soon be heaving with 60,000

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:41.260
elephant seals but they won't have the beaches to themselves one month later

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:52.600
another wave of migrants appear in the surf these are royal penguins and they're

00:08:52.600 --> 00:09:00.000
about to have their first sighting of land in seven months they've spent that

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.000
hunting for fish and shrimp in the open ocean.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:20.800
One last hurdle and they're back on terra firma.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:29.030
This island is actually the only solid ground these penguins will ever set foot on.

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:39.120
Macquarie's nearest neighbor is more than 400 miles away

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:44.010
and in the opposite direction from their favorite feeding grounds.

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:59.190
Within days of the first arrivals, it's standing room only.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:09.940
Nobody knows exactly how these penguins

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:12.060
navigate back to Macquarie,

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:15.900
but with this many adults returning every year to breed,

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:17.220
it clearly works.

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:52.900
Out in the open ocean, royal penguins lead solitary lives,

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:56.150
so these cramped conditions take a bit of getting used to.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:07.660
Dispute settled, it's time to renew old acquaintances.

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:17.550
The elephant seals have also settled down, even if some still play hard to get, though

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:22.270
it's not easy turning down the advances of a suitor six times your size.

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:30.000
A gentle nuzzle, and she appears wide.

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:44.230
And it's not just love that's in the air.

00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:49.910
At these southerly latitudes, ocean winds bring rain six and a half days out of seven.

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:55.740
And when it doesn't rain, it snows.

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:59.890
It couldn't be more different from the cliched image of a South Pacific island.

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:06.190
But surprisingly, what makes Macquarie so rich in wildlife, also benefits other islands

00:12:06.190 --> 00:12:10.920
in the tropical South Pacific, thousands of miles away.

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:20.150
Macquarie's penguins and seals depend on a cold, nutrient-rich current, and that doesn't

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:25.020
stop here.

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:30.000
Powered by the strongest winds in the world, the current continues east until it hits the

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:44.620
of South America where it's driven northwards. Over 8,000 miles later, the cold current reaches

00:12:44.620 --> 00:13:00.000
the equator and a remote archipelago. The water has warmed up on the journey north, but it's still cold

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:03.320
nutrient rich.

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:07.860
This has allowed some animals to live here, which you wouldn't normally find this close

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:15.800
to the equator.

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:16.640
Sea lions.

00:13:30.890 --> 00:13:35.890
Isolated on the Galapagos, they've been here so long they've become a separate species.

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:55.180
When the surf's up, the sea lions spend hours just messing around in the waves.

00:13:55.180 --> 00:14:00.000
Surfing burns a lot of energy.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:12.240
and they can only do it because these cold seas support vast numbers of fish.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:15.860
The sea lions share these nutrient-rich waters

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:23.710
with another equatorial misfit:

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:29.320
penguins, the only penguins found in the tropics.

00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:38.770
Like the sea lions, Galapagos penguins can survive here

00:14:38.770 --> 00:14:51.200
because the water keeps them cool and well fed.

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:55.030
But these conditions are not constant.

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:57.940
In El Nino years, when currents reverse,

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:00.000
warm waters replace cold.

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:04.280
fish populations crash and then stuck on Galapagos,

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:11.940
penguins and sea lions starve.

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:13.760
Their numbers will bounce back,

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:16.140
but it's the price these animals pay for life

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:26.720
on these isolated islands.

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:29.510
There are thousands of islands in the South Pacific.

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:35.890
Many are unbelievably remote.

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:43.130
Some are thousands of miles away from the nearest continent

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:46.530
and hundreds of miles from their nearest neighbor.

00:15:46.530 --> 00:15:54.420
Imagine each island as a castle

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:59.640
and the ocean a giant moat stretching to the horizon in every direction.

00:16:00.090 --> 00:16:08.240
you have captured the essence of their isolation. It's a wonder how any life

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:25.660
reached these islands at all. No matter how remote or small an island is, animals

00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:30.000
have somehow conquered the massive ocean barrier to reach these special

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:32.360
of land.

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:43.360
The lucky few that made it, South Pacific Islands provided great opportunities.

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:57.140
Once here, they had the freedom to be different.

00:16:57.140 --> 00:16:59.030
Crabs may not seem unusual.

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:03.110
But there's one kind here that's like no other.

00:17:03.110 --> 00:17:10.850
On this little island in Vanuatu lives a real oddity.

00:17:10.850 --> 00:17:23.130
It's the largest terrestrial invertebrate on Earth.

00:17:35.050 --> 00:17:38.520
the robber crab

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:41.650
the biggest can weigh up to four kilograms

00:17:41.650 --> 00:17:44.030
the same as a newborn baby

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:47.500
and have a leg span of one meter

00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:53.130
it's a hermit crab on steroids

00:17:53.130 --> 00:17:54.050
at night

00:17:54.050 --> 00:17:56.500
the huge robbers really come alive

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:17.360
Although these crabs are found throughout the Pacific, it's only on undisturbed islands

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:23.190
that you can see them in such numbers.

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:30.000
Exactly why robber crabs have grown so big is a mystery, but with so few creatures making

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:35.570
these remote islands. The giant crustaceans seem to have filled a niche normally taken

00:18:35.570 --> 00:18:45.060
by medium-sized mammals. And with almost no competition from other native animals, the

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:50.580
crab's huge size allows them to make the most of another great colonizer, something

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:52.370
found here in abundance.

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:08.630
coconuts are one of their favorite foods it's why they're also called coconut

00:19:08.630 --> 00:19:12.590
crabs and they've been known to carry them as far as three miles back to a

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:30.000
favorite then robber crabs are perhaps the only animals in the world aged

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:37.780
able to break into a coconut. The husk is ripped off by powerful pincers. Other legs

00:19:37.780 --> 00:19:48.930
drill through the germinating holes on the seed itself until the nut finally cracks. The

00:19:48.930 --> 00:20:00.000
whole process can take several hours, but the reward is a meal rich in protein. Robber

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:12.700
rule the land but they're no masters of the sea an adult crab would drown in a

00:20:12.700 --> 00:20:20.010
few minutes which raises the question how did these monsters get to so many of

00:20:20.010 --> 00:20:28.390
the South Pacific's most isolated islands this female carries the answer

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:44.710
Thousands of eggs which will soon be left to the mercy of the ocean currents.

00:20:44.710 --> 00:20:51.760
A few shakes of her tail are all that's needed to send them on their way.

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:54.050
The eggs will hatch immediately.

00:20:54.050 --> 00:20:58.900
Then the larvae will have just 50 days to find a new home above the water.

00:21:05.790 --> 00:21:12.170
For animal castaways, finding new land in this vast ocean was a chance in a million.

00:21:12.170 --> 00:21:16.530
To beat the odds, luck was needed.

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:24.280
And this sometimes came from an unlikely source: cyclones.

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:29.960
Each year, these powerful tropical storms form over the huge ocean.

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:44.760
The largest can span more than 600 miles.

00:22:03.290 --> 00:22:07.050
Cyclones are one of the most destructive forces in the South Pacific.

00:22:07.770 --> 00:22:12.830
Yet surprisingly, they have played a critical role in spreading life to the loneliest islands.

00:22:15.290 --> 00:22:19.840
And few islands are more remote than these.

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:30.000
Thousand miles from the nearest continent, Hawaii is the world's world.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:39.290
most isolated archipelago. It's so far flung that less than 500 kinds of animals settled

00:22:39.290 --> 00:22:47.180
here in 30 million years. Remarkably, many of these colonizers were carried to Hawaii

00:22:47.180 --> 00:23:00.000
on the back of cyclones. For an animal to be sucked up by storm winds carried across

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:08.300
the ocean and dumped here alive was a matter of extraordinary luck. Yet that's what happened

00:23:08.300 --> 00:23:21.710
to the ancestor of this insect. It's a kind of fruit fly, but no ordinary one. Hawaii's

00:23:21.710 --> 00:23:30.000
fruit flies are the birds of paradise of the insect world. They attract females with elaborate courtship

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:37.050
rituals, and have a sophisticated range of territorial behavior.

00:23:37.050 --> 00:23:40.870
Male hammerhead flies use their heads as battering rams.

00:23:40.870 --> 00:23:53.590
A male claviceti fruit fly fans sex pheromones from his raised abdomen.

00:23:53.590 --> 00:23:58.040
His extended tongue is an added attraction.

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:09.990
But since that first colonizer, they have evolved into nearly a thousand species, many

00:24:09.990 --> 00:24:13.120
with their own unique behavior.

00:24:31.260 --> 00:24:40.240
when it comes to choosing a mate females are very fussy one wrong move by her suitor

00:24:40.240 --> 00:24:59.060
and she's off eyes isolation has had a curious effect on the evolution of some of its other wildlife too

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:04.890
Crawling around these ferns are caterpillars.

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:12.700
They're the larvae of a moth and look ordinary enough.

00:25:12.700 --> 00:25:22.570
But these tiny caterpillars are perhaps the strangest of their kind in the world.

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:26.790
When one finds the right spot, it settles down to chew through a leaf.

00:25:31.440 --> 00:25:44.090
So far, so normal, but the caterpillar is not actually swallowing the bits of leaf.

00:25:44.090 --> 00:25:51.640
It's channeling a gap between the segments.

00:25:51.640 --> 00:25:58.710
When finished, it'll tuck itself into the space.

00:25:58.710 --> 00:26:00.000
So what is so strange?

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:07.150
about this animal well those are not your typical caterpillar feet and what follows is not your

00:26:07.150 --> 00:26:23.560
typical caterpillar behavior this is a carnivorous caterpillar with a vice-like hold and a bite to match

00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:36.080
Hawaii's numerous fruit flies were just too good an opportunity to ignore.

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:44.800
And the stick-like camouflage of another kind of meat-eating caterpillar

00:26:44.800 --> 00:26:48.050
is just as effective when ambushing prey.

00:27:01.050 --> 00:27:06.750
Nobody knows what set Hawaii's carnivorous caterpillars on this extraordinary path,

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:16.030
but it's the sort of quirky evolution that's common on isolated islands.

00:27:16.030 --> 00:27:24.780
Each South Pacific island has its own unique set of creatures.

00:27:24.780 --> 00:27:30.000
With so many islands, this adds up to thousands of animals found nowhere else on

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:45.070
Earth. Isolated by miles of ocean, human colonizers also developed different ways of life. Each

00:27:45.070 --> 00:27:58.840
culture has its own customs, and some are truly bizarre. The locals are in celebratory mood,

00:28:09.630 --> 00:28:23.510
This man is about to perform one of the strangest rituals in the world.

00:28:23.510 --> 00:28:27.680
It's a tradition that tests the mettle of the most courageous men.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:32.820
Having a head for heights is only the start.

00:28:39.630 --> 00:28:42.270
Forest vines are tied around his ankles.

00:28:44.320 --> 00:28:45.840
They will be his lifeline.

00:29:20.540 --> 00:29:24.550
For centuries, Pentecost men have been leaping headfirst

00:29:24.550 --> 00:29:27.940
from wooden scaffolds with only forest vines

00:29:27.940 --> 00:29:29.350
to break their fall.

00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:36.760
A tradition that inspired modern bungee jumping.

00:30:19.840 --> 00:30:30.000
The jumps may look like acts of madness, but the festival's origins have a serious side.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:32.580
the annual harvest of their staple crop.

00:30:33.340 --> 00:30:36.860
And with up to nine cyclones pounding the South Pacific a year,

00:30:37.270 --> 00:30:40.230
a successful harvest is worth celebrating.

00:31:01.980 --> 00:31:06.260
The close calls with Mother Earth are not miscalculations.

00:31:06.260 --> 00:31:09.340
The diver's hair is actually meant to brush the ground

00:31:09.340 --> 00:31:19.150
in a symbolic act of fertilization.

00:31:19.150 --> 00:31:22.670
And it's believed the closer the jumper gets to the ground,

00:31:22.670 --> 00:31:25.380
the taller the crops will grow the next year.

00:31:48.080 --> 00:31:52.720
human history across most of the south pacific dates back less than two

00:31:52.720 --> 00:32:00.000
thousand years by comparison animal colonizers first arrived on these shores

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:08.090
over 30 million years ago yet by the 12th century people had colonized almost every habitable piece

00:32:08.090 --> 00:32:22.310
of land surrounded by water the ocean became embedded in their culture sometimes in surprising

00:32:22.310 --> 00:32:23.110
ways

00:32:32.780 --> 00:32:44.600
In the Banks Islands, women use the sea to make music and their songs celebrate the creatures that live in it.

00:32:44.600 --> 00:32:59.350
No matter where people settled in the South Pacific, their survival depended on a deep understanding of the ocean and its wildlife.

00:33:14.560 --> 00:33:20.060
And there's one ocean event that has featured in the Pacific Islanders' calendar since they first arrived here.

00:33:24.010 --> 00:33:26.710
It happens on just one night a year in November.

00:33:30.280 --> 00:33:39.810
Armed with torches and homemade nets, these islanders prepare for a harvest.

00:33:39.810 --> 00:33:44.630
They gather in the reef shallows, watching and waiting for what will soon be a flurry

00:33:44.630 --> 00:33:50.700
of activity.

00:33:50.700 --> 00:33:56.600
As the moon rises, it triggers a natural phenomenon and a very strange spectacle.

00:34:04.540 --> 00:34:06.760
The rising of worms.

00:34:06.760 --> 00:34:09.060
Palolo worms.

00:34:09.060 --> 00:34:13.210
These are actually the worms' rear ends.

00:34:13.210 --> 00:34:15.450
They're reproductive segments.

00:34:15.450 --> 00:34:21.210
The part with the head remains in the coral rock.

00:34:21.210 --> 00:34:26.240
When they reach the surface, eggs and sperm will mix.

00:34:43.720 --> 00:34:49.200
The tide washes the wriggling worms into the shallows, where they are scooped up by the bucketful.

00:34:49.200 --> 00:35:00.000
The whole event lasts just a couple of hours, but in that time hundreds of kilos can be

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:17.590
gathered rich in proteins and fats halola worms are the caviar of the south pacific this may be

00:35:17.590 --> 00:35:23.400
a short-lived feast but living in the south pacific means making the most of anything going

00:35:34.220 --> 00:35:40.640
every year in june tiger sharks appear in the shallow waters around this scattering of tiny

00:35:40.640 --> 00:35:51.780
sandy islands they've traveled hundreds of miles in anticipation of an event that lasts

00:35:51.780 --> 00:35:57.720
less than two weeks their timing is impeccable

00:36:07.840 --> 00:36:16.180
Albatross chicks are sitting ducks.

00:36:16.180 --> 00:36:19.360
The sharks may have lost the element of surprise,

00:36:19.360 --> 00:36:21.800
but their high visibility will make little difference

00:36:21.800 --> 00:36:26.650
to the albatross chicks' behavior.

00:36:26.650 --> 00:36:29.090
Right now, the focus is on flying.

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:39.040
For fledging albatross, that's not as simple as you'd think.

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:45.860
The problem is the island's runway is a bit short,

00:36:45.860 --> 00:36:49.790
especially for a bird with one of the world's longest wingspans.

00:36:49.790 --> 00:36:54.480
The maiden flight often ends in a wet landing,

00:36:54.480 --> 00:36:58.290
and that's why the sharks are here.

00:37:02.490 --> 00:37:07.490
With their highly acute senses, they're onto the chicks within a few moments of touchdown.

00:37:07.490 --> 00:37:26.910
During two weeks of fledging, over a hundred albatross chicks make the same fatal error.

00:37:26.910 --> 00:37:30.000
And the sharks take full advantage.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:04.620
ocean where food can be surprisingly scarce this annual feast is something

00:38:04.620 --> 00:38:18.640
these sharks depend on the size of many South Pacific Islands means that food is

00:38:18.640 --> 00:38:25.100
often limited the smaller and more isolated they are the harder it is to

00:38:25.100 --> 00:38:29.800
survive and in this ocean of islands

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:35.630
there's one tiny island that stands out.

00:38:35.630 --> 00:38:38.030
It's home to perhaps the most remote community

00:38:38.030 --> 00:38:39.810
of people on the planet,

00:38:39.810 --> 00:38:42.100
with a truly inspiring story.

00:39:05.080 --> 00:39:12.540
These men are from the island of Anuta.

00:39:12.540 --> 00:39:16.070
This fishing technique is unique to their island

00:39:16.070 --> 00:39:23.140
and essential when the weather is too rough for their boats.

00:39:23.140 --> 00:39:30.000
Octopus tentacles are used as bait and it's very

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:37.250
effective once a show has been found dozens of fish can be caught in this way

00:39:37.250 --> 00:39:52.850
the struggling fish are killed with a quick bite to the head

00:40:00.190 --> 00:40:05.030
Anutans have more fishing techniques than almost anywhere else in the Pacific.

00:40:05.030 --> 00:40:08.130
A vital skill given the size of their island.

00:40:08.130 --> 00:40:20.610
And this is it, just one sixth of a square mile in area.

00:40:20.610 --> 00:40:29.700
Anuta is so small that no matter where you are, the sound of the waves is ever present.

00:40:38.570 --> 00:40:44.250
Life on Anuta has changed little since these people's ancestors arrived here nearly 400

00:40:44.250 --> 00:40:47.840
years ago.

